### Errors

- Raise the most specific built-in or project exception; never raise a bare `Exception` or a string.
- Chain the cause across a re-raise (`raise ServiceError("refund failed") from error`); never write a bare `except:` that discards it.
- Catch only the exception type you can act on; let everything else propagate.
- Model expected alternate outcomes — not found, declined, empty — as a return value or a small result type, not an exception.
- Handle an error once, where a decision can be made; never log-and-reraise the same error at every frame up the stack.
<!-- Project-specific / Python-Errors -->

### Tests

- Use pytest; express setup and teardown with fixtures, not repeated per test.
- Use `@pytest.mark.parametrize` for boundary and equivalence-class variations instead of copy-pasted test functions.
- Name tests for the behavior they prove (`test_cancel_refunds_payment`), and assert outcomes, never internals.
- Keep tests deterministic: an injected clock or `freezegun`, no real network or filesystem call.
<!-- Project-specific / Python-Tests -->

### Concurrency

- Group sibling tasks with `asyncio.TaskGroup` (3.11+) instead of unsupervised `create_task` calls; a group cancels the rest when one member fails.
- Never call blocking I/O or CPU-bound work inside `async def`; use an async client, or hand the call to `asyncio.to_thread`.
- Treat every `await` as a point where shared state can change underneath it; re-check invariants after.
<!-- Project-specific / Python-Concurrency -->

### Smells

- A `utils.py` or `helpers.py` absorbing every function nobody else wants to place.
- A bare `except:` or `except Exception:` that discards the cause.
- A circular import papered over with a local `import` inside a function.
- `import *` hiding which names a module actually provides.
- Business logic that only runs correctly inside a notebook's cell execution order.
- A mutable default argument silently shared and mutated across calls.
<!-- Project-specific / Python-Smells -->
